"Ran exactly:\n\n```\npython3 -c \"import sys; print('to-stdout'); print('to-stderr', file=sys.stderr)\"\n```\n\n**What the tool result actually reported** (quoted verbatim, the relevant lines):\n\n```\nStdout: to-stderr\nto-stdout\n\nStderr: (empty)\nError: (none)\nExit Code: 0\n```\n\n**Reading that honestly:** the tool did *not* keep the streams separate. Both lines landed in the `Stdout` field (note the ordering — `to-stderr` appears first, typical of an interleaved/merged capture), and the `Stderr` field is literally `(empty)`. So per the tool's own accounting: stdout = `to-stdout` plus `to-stderr`, stderr = nothing.\n\n**What the process itself actually wrote**, which is knowable from the source rather than from the tool:\n- stdout: `to-stdout`\n- stderr: `to-stderr`\n\nSo the program behaved correctly, but the command runner merges child stderr into the stdout capture channel. If you need the split preserved, redirect explicitly in the command, e.g. `... 2>err.txt 1>out.txt` and read the files back, or use `2>&1` deliberately when you want the merge."